from __future__ import annotations

from thinking_core import render_long, render_note


def pilot_to_scale() -> str:
    body = '''<p>A pilot answers one question: <strong>can this work?</strong></p><p>Scale answers a harder one: <strong>can the organization own it, operate it, govern it and create repeatable value from it without depending on the original project team?</strong></p><p>I have seen technically successful AI initiatives lose momentum precisely after the demo. The technology had done its job. The operating conditions around it had not been built.</p><blockquote>Technical success is evidence of possibility. Enterprise scale is evidence of operating capability.</blockquote><h2>The real gap appears after the pilot</h2><p>In enterprise AI portfolios and CoE work, the same questions surface repeatedly once experimentation becomes a production decision.</p><p>Who owns the business outcome? What evidence justifies the next investment? Who owns the data at production standard? What happens when performance changes? Who redesigns the workflow? Who carries accountability once the initial team moves on?</p><p>During a pilot, postponing some of these questions can be sensible. The purpose is to learn quickly. The mistake is assuming that a successful experiment has already answered them.</p><p>This is where AI leadership changes from encouraging experimentation to making operating choices.</p><h2>The trade-off is not governance versus speed</h2><p>I would not put production-level structure around every early experiment. That slows learning and creates controls before the organization even knows what deserves to scale.</p><p>But waiting until a pilot succeeds creates the opposite problem: a portfolio of impressive demonstrations with no credible route into operations.</p><p>The better approach is <strong>staged evidence</strong>. As an initiative asks for more funding, more users, more sensitive data or greater influence over real decisions, the evidence required from it should rise as well.</p><p>Before I would call an AI use case scale-ready, I want five things to be clear:</p><p><strong>1. A real business owner.</strong><br>Accountability for the outcome cannot sit permanently with the AI team or a project sponsor.</p><p><strong>2. A credible value mechanism.</strong><br>We should know which workflow, decision or behavior is expected to change, and how that change connects to a business outcome.</p><p><strong>3. Production-grade data ownership.</strong><br>Pilot access is not the same as sustained accountability for quality, semantics, access and monitoring.</p><p><strong>4. Controls proportionate to risk.</strong><br>Human oversight, escalation and evidence should reflect the consequences of the use case, not a generic governance template.</p><p><strong>5. An operating model that survives the project.</strong><br>If the solution only works while the original enthusiasts are present, we have extended the pilot, not scaled a capability.</p><h2>The CoE should enable scale, not become the owner of AI</h2><p>A central AI or Data team should provide reusable methods, specialist expertise, common guardrails and clear scale-readiness criteria. It should not become the permanent home of every use case.</p><p>I prefer a federated model: central expertise where common capability or risk requires it, business ownership where value and workflow accountability belong, and explicit hand-offs between the two.</p><p>That is the leadership test I care about between pilot and scale.</p><div class="rule"><strong>Do not scale the demo. Scale the operating capability around it.</strong></div>'''
    aside = '''<span>Scale-readiness check</span><strong>Five questions before the next investment decision.</strong><div class="article-questions"><div>Who owns the business outcome?</div><div>What value mechanism are we testing?</div><div>Who owns production data quality and semantics?</div><div>What evidence and controls match the risk?</div><div>Who operates this after the project team leaves?</div></div><p>This is the practical distinction I make between proving an AI use case and building an enterprise capability.</p>'''
    return render_long("pilot_to_scale", body, aside)


def governance_accountability() -> str:
    return render_note(
        "governance_accountability",
        "Governance can be lighter. Accountability cannot disappear.",
        "A risk review can be simplified, automated or tiered. Documentation can change with the level of exposure. But before an AI use case influences a real business decision at scale, I want one thing to be unambiguous: a human owner for the outcome. Someone must know what the system is meant to influence, what evidence makes its use acceptable, what should be monitored and when the organization should stop or intervene. My rule is simple: if nobody can answer ‘who is accountable when this changes a real decision?’, the use case is not ready to scale.",
    )
